# Finite request/lookup sequence binder

`sequence.py` relocates existing reversible request and lookup circuits into one
explicit logical address space. It retains distinct original input and output
registers for every call, shares one chronological log and counter, and reuses
only workspace that each component returns clean. The concrete lazy stream is
emitted, rather than replacing component execution with a metadata subtotal.

The sequence is selected classically in advance. It is not an adaptive controller,
an input preparer, a descriptor or witness validator, or a complete reduction.
The frozen component contracts are premises. Instantiating their classes alone
does not authenticate an arbitrary supplied generator/kernel's semantics.
No production-size stream has been executed. Production remains gated.

## Implemented boundary

Supported component classes are the accepted `RequestTransition`, bounded
`ContentRequest`, common-result `ContentFamily`, fixed out-of-range
`RejectedVoteFamily`, and `OverlayLookup`. Component result encodings are retained
exactly: the binder does not claim to unify accepted-only and common-result
interfaces. It counts all original inputs, including coins and borrowed rejected
lists. It does not duplicate fields when routing a component: wire relocation is
a static injective renaming, under the premise that the caller supplies those
specific disjoint registers directly. Preparing or moving real inputs into them
remains outside this component and is not assigned zero cost.

Every component's entire local address space must partition into disjoint mapped
intervals. Shared log geometry must agree exactly. A fixed-length F instruction
renames its explicit wire operands. The variable-length F instruction separately
renames its three intervals while retaining the length width, message capacity
and response width as metadata. An interval crossing a relocation boundary is
refused. This prevents treating a capacity integer as a wire address or truncating
the full query to the stored-key width. All calls use the same supplied immutable
F and total completion; identity strings merely express the caller's assertion.
Smaller response widths belong to synthetic oracle fixtures, never a free
adapter to the real 2112-bit F interface.

Request calls retain generated values until programming/return are complete,
then clear derived work. The inverse reverses event order and uses each request's
true append inverse. Repeating a request's forward map is not an inverse.
The lookup uses its clean XOR map again as inverse, only on its clean-workspace
subspace and with the same immutable F. None of this permits interleaving inside
an atomic component or adversarial writes to log, counter, private coins or work.

The initial log/counter and shared scratch must be zero. Each generating call
reserves its full M+2 possible appends, including invalid content whose actual
validity the binder does not inspect. Prefix reservations must fit capacity.
Rejected-size calls reserve zero. Reservations do not compute actual occupancy.
Here request count includes invalid requests. It is not the source theorem's
valid-request-only T; a full driver must separately bound and charge other calls.

## Validation scope

Owned controls execute reduced synthetic generators and real orchestration,
checking full-stream versus per-call equality, newest duplicate wins, base misses,
disabled queries, invalid-content and size-rejection log identity, arbitrary XOR
targets, retained inputs, clean workspace and exact inverse recovery. A mutation
control demonstrates that a second forward append changes the log instead of
undoing it. Separate interval tests use a nontrivial permutation of global
locations and compare fixed/variable F semantics. Malformed intervals, aliases,
types, capacities and excess reservations are refused.

A source-bound zero-capacity L0 plus a rejected VOTE of size2^64-1 demonstrates
constant-size metadata construction with the full borrowed input included in
logical storage; only a short gate prefix is emitted. This is not a test that
allocates an enormous native list or executes its complete stream.

Any captured validation applies to its exact frozen source inventory. The live
checkout currently differs in the ballot crate's Cargo manifest; that change is
not silently accepted by repinning prior evidence. Python, pytest, source semantic
contracts and the selected snapshot loader remain trusted. Neither a source hash
inventory nor the tests establish a compiler/runtime dependency closure.

